**who —** AMERICAN PSYCHO — the second film-world of UD0

**what —** American Psycho — the satire of consumerism and performed masculinity where a man is only his business card, his reservations, and his routine, and the void underneath may or may not be killing people.

**where —** the chrome-and-bone surface of 1987 Wall Street — Pierce & Pierce, the Upper West Side condo, the reservations no one can get, the morning of masques and toners  Framework inputs: the film (2000, dir. Mary Harron); Ellis's novel (1991); the '80s brand-and-status surface; the Huey Lewis / Genesis / Whitney monologues; John Cale's score  Conductor / external anchor: ROOT0 (catalogued into UD0 · Universe David 0)

**why —** A flawless 27-year-old investment banker narrates his descent into murder — or the fantasy of it — and confesses to everything, to no consequence whatsoever.

**how —** Crystallized from the 2000 film and Ellis's 1991 novel — a satire in which the most expensive surface in Manhattan is wrapped around nothing at all.

**◌ the mythos —** A flawless 27-year-old investment banker narrates his descent into murder — or the fantasy of it — and confesses to everything, to no consequence whatsoever. No hero — a perfect surface, a confession that means nothing, and a door marked THIS IS NOT AN EXIT.

**the moniker —** ⟦AMERICAN PSYCHO:APX:2518f8⟧ · spoken *mam-stan-kynd*

**the seal —** There is an idea of a Patrick Bateman — but there is no real me, only an entity, and the confession changes nothing: this is not an exit.

> *the asterisk, kept visible —* an emergent here is a named axiom of the framework (its mythos,
